    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A pocket door is the better door and the harder project. It closes against a jamb, takes a real privacy latch and leaves the wall clear, but it needs an empty wall cavity at least as wide as the door beside the opening and, in an existing house, that usually means opening the wall. A barn door goes up in an afternoon on almost any wall with framing behind it, and gives up sealing, privacy and wall space in return. In new construction the pocket door usually wins. In a retrofit, the barn door usually does.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pocket doors and barn doors solve the same problem, which is a hinged door's swing arc eating floor space, in opposite ways. A pocket door slides into a hollow cavity built inside the wall. When open, it is invisible apart from the edge and a pull. When closed, it sits inside the opening and meets a jamb on the latch side. A barn door hangs from an exposed track above the opening and slides across the face of the wall. When open, it covers a stretch of wall. When closed, it hangs in front of the opening with a gap all the way round.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything else follows from that difference. The pocket door is more private, quieter, cleaner looking and kinder to furniture placement, because the wall it slides into stays usable. The barn door is far easier to fit into an existing house, easier to maintain, easier to grip and a deliberate design statement. Our <a href="/best-barn-doors/">barn door buying guide</a> is candid about the barn door's limits, and they are the same limits that make the pocket door attractive.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pocket door</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Barn door</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where the door goes when open</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inside the wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">On the face of the wall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall beside the opening</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hollow cavity, must be empty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface must be clear</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Closes against a jamb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, on the latch side</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, hangs in front</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Privacy latch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, pocket door locks exist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hooks and surface latches only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Install in an existing wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Open the wall, new header, refinish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Track on studs or a backer board</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical DIY difficulty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard in a retrofit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repairs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Track is hidden in the wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Everything is exposed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are framing new walls, specify the pocket door where you want a real door and the floor space matters. If you are working with finished walls, start by asking what is inside the wall where the pocket would go. Plumbing, wiring, ductwork or a load-bearing wall without a suitable header can turn a pocket door from a moderate job into a major one, which is usually the moment a barn door starts to look sensible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wall-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Wall Space: Inside the Wall or On It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both doors need a stretch of wall beside the opening at least as wide as the door. The difference is which part of the wall they need. A barn door needs the surface: no light switches, outlets, thermostats, vents or return corners in the path, and nothing hung on that stretch of wall that you are unwilling to move. A pocket door needs the inside: the cavity has to be empty of studs, which the frame kit replaces, and of anything else that normally runs through a wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            That second requirement is the one people underestimate. There can be no plumbing, no wiring and no ductwork in the pocket. Switches and outlets cannot sit on either face of the wall over the pocket, because their boxes would project into the door's path. If the obvious place for a light switch beside a bedroom door is exactly where the pocket would go, the switch has to move to the other side of the opening, and that is electrical work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once built, a pocket wall is also a weaker wall. Pocket door frames replace the studs with split studs, slim uprights either side of the door, so the wall cannot carry heavy shelving, a wall-mounted TV or cabinets over the pocket. Drywall has to be fixed with screws short enough not to protrude into the cavity, or they scrape the door every time it moves. Plan the room's furniture and fixtures with that in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            The barn door's space cost is visible rather than hidden. Its track needs headroom above the opening for the rail and rollers, and the door stands off the wall by roughly an inch plus its own thickness, which can foul an adjacent door casing or a piece of furniture. But the wall itself keeps its full strength, and you can still use it for anything that does not sit in the door's travel.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A barn door needs the surface of the wall. A pocket door needs the inside.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Before choosing, walk the wall beside the opening and find out what runs inside it. Switches, outlets, pipes and ducts in that stretch are a barn door problem on the surface and a pocket door problem everywhere.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Installation Actually Involves
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pocket door is installed as a frame kit before the wall is closed up. The kit usually provides a header with an integrated track, split-stud uprights, floor brackets and hangers. The rough opening is a little over twice the width of the door, because the door needs room to slide entirely into the wall. Retailer listings for Johnson Hardware's 1500 Series frame for a 30 inch door, for example, give a framed rough opening of 61 by 84-1/2 inches in a standard 2 by 4 wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            That wider opening needs a header to match. In a non-structural partition wall that is straightforward carpentry. In a load-bearing wall, the header over the whole rough opening has to be sized to carry the load above, and that is a question for an engineer or your building department, usually with a permit. Plenty of pocket door projects stall at exactly this point once the wall is opened.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight and door thickness are set by the kit. The same retailer listings rate the standard 1500 Series for doors up to 125 pounds and the heavier 1500HD for solid-core doors up to 200 pounds, with door thickness typically up to 1-3/8 inches unless an adaptor kit is used. Check the specific model against your door before ordering, because a solid-core or glass pocket door can exceed a light-duty frame's rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            A barn door is a surface job. The track mounts above the opening into studs or onto a backer board fixed across several studs, the door hangs from rollers, and a floor guide keeps the bottom against the wall. A competent DIYer with a drill, a long level and a helper for the lift can usually do it in an afternoon. The critical step is finding solid framing, which is why a <a href="/best-stud-finder/">reliable stud finder</a> earns its price on this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The skill gap is real. A pocket door involves framing, drywall, taping, trim and paint, and usually electrical work in a retrofit. A barn door involves locating studs and getting a track dead level. If you would hire out drywall work, you should probably hire out a retrofit pocket door, and that changes the cost comparison considerably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="retrofit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Retrofit vs New Build
          </h2>

          <p>
            In new construction, a pocket door is an easy decision. The wall is open anyway, the frame kit goes in with the rest of the framing, and the electrician and plumber simply route around the pocket. The extra cost over a standard door is mostly the kit and the door, and the result is a cleaner room with more usable floor space.
          </p>

          <p>
            In an existing house, the pocket door becomes a renovation. The wall beside the opening has to be opened on at least one face, often both. Studs come out, the header is rebuilt to span the wider opening, any wiring is rerouted, the frame kit goes in, and then the wall is closed up, taped, finished, painted and trimmed. If you are patching around the work yourself, our guide to <a href="/joint-compound-vs-spackle/">joint compound vs spackle</a> explains which filler each repair needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Published cost guides show the gap clearly. HomeAdvisor's pocket door guide, updated in June 2026, lists a 36 inch pocket door at roughly $550 to $1,850 installed in new construction against $1,550 to $2,950 as a remodel, and puts the cost of opening up a wall for a pocket door at $1,200 to $2,500 on its own. Fixr's guide, updated in January 2025, estimates that retrofitting adds roughly $900 to $1,800 for most door materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            A barn door is the opposite. It is designed for finished walls, needs no demolition and leaves the wall intact, which is why it became popular as a retrofit. The work is the same in a new house or an old one, provided there is framing to fix into and clear wall beside the opening. That makes it the natural choice where a pocket door would mean opening a wall with services in it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A middle path is worth knowing. If the wall beside the opening is already being opened for another reason, such as rewiring or a bathroom remodel, the incremental cost of a pocket door falls sharply because the demolition and refinishing are already in the budget. That is the best moment to switch a hinged door to a pocket door in an existing house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="privacy-sound" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Privacy, Sound and Locking
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pocket door is the more private of the two by a clear margin. It slides inside the opening and closes against a jamb on the latch side, so there is no open gap around three sides as there is with a barn door. It also accepts proper pocket door hardware, including privacy locks that work like a bathroom lock on a hinged door. For bathrooms, bedrooms and offices, that alone often decides the question.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is not a sealed door, though. There are small clearances where the door enters the pocket and at the floor, and many pocket doors are hollow-core slabs that pass sound readily. A solid-core door and careful trim help. Owners who need real acoustic separation, such as a home office used for calls, are still usually better served by a solid hinged door with weatherstripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            A barn door hangs in front of the opening, with a gap of around half an inch at the sides and a clearance gap at the bottom. Sound, light and smells pass through freely, and the available locks are hooks and surface latches that signal privacy rather than provide it. That is acceptable on a pantry, a laundry, a closet or an ensuite off a bedroom, and a poor fit on a shared bathroom or a bedroom off a living area.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the look of a sliding door but want light rather than privacy, a glass-paned barn door is worth considering for openings such as offices and dens. Our guide to <a href="/french-barn-doors/">French barn doors</a> covers how the glass type changes the privacy trade-off. And if you are weighing a sliding door against hinged doors altogether, the <a href="/barn-door-vs-french-door/">barn door vs French door comparison</a> covers that decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accessibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Accessibility and Everyday Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both doors avoid a swing arc, which is a genuine advantage for wheelchair and walker users in tight spaces, and both can provide a full clear opening because the door moves entirely out of the way. The 2010 ADA Standards, which govern public buildings rather than private homes but are a useful benchmark, require a clear width of at least 32 inches and limit the force to push or pull open a sliding door to 5 pounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardware is where the two differ. Pocket doors traditionally use flush pulls recessed into the face of the door, plus a small edge pull to draw the door out of the pocket. Recessed pulls are hard to use with limited grip strength. The ADA Standards address this directly: where sliding doors are in the fully open position, operating hardware must be exposed and usable from both sides. For a pocket door, that means a door stop that leaves the edge pull accessible, or hardware designed for accessibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            A barn door's handles are surface-mounted and large, which is easier for most hands. The weight works against it, though. A heavy solid or glass slab takes more effort to start moving, and soft-close dampers, which most people want for noise, add resistance at the ends of travel. Lighter doors and well-adjusted hardware make a real difference for anyone with limited strength.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance and repairs</h3>

          <p>
            Barn door hardware is completely exposed, which makes it easy to inspect, adjust and replace. The common faults are fixings loosening in the first weeks, a floor guide working loose, and soft-close dampers needing adjustment. All are fixed with a spanner, and a door can be lifted off its track by two people in minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pocket door hardware lives inside the wall, so most problems happen out of sight. Many frame kits are designed so the door can be lifted off its hangers after removing a strip of trim, and hangers can then be adjusted or replaced. The expensive failures are a bent track or a damaged split stud inside the wall, often caused by something heavy hitting the wall or long screws used when hanging pictures. Those can mean opening the wall again. Buying a well-rated frame kit is cheap insurance against that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Pocket Door vs Barn Door Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Are pocket doors more expensive than regular doors? Yes, according to the major cost guides, and the gap is widest in existing walls. HomeAdvisor's 2026 figures put pocket door installation at an average of about $1,050, with $550 to $3,300 in new construction, compared with $150 to $200 for a standard door on the same page. Fixr's comparison lists a traditional interior door at about $350 to $1,100 installed and a pocket door at about $800 to $2,500.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Published Installed Cost Ranges</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$800-$2,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pocket door (Fixr, 2025)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$600-$2,000</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Barn door (Fixr, 2025)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1,550-$2,950</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">36 in pocket door, remodel (HomeAdvisor, 2026)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$350-$1,100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standard interior door (Fixr, 2025)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against a barn door, Fixr's 2025 pocket door guide puts a barn door at roughly $600 to $2,000 installed, overlapping heavily with a pocket door in new construction. The difference opens up in a retrofit, where the pocket door carries demolition, header work, drywall finishing and often electrical work. A barn door's cost is mostly the door and hardware, and the main cost decision is how much to spend on a quality track and rollers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat all of these as national ranges from cost-guide publishers, not quotes. They vary from year to year and between sources, and your price depends on local labour rates, whether the wall is load-bearing, what is inside it and how much finishing work is needed. Get two or three local quotes for a retrofit pocket door, and ask each contractor what they expect to find in the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The value calculation is simpler than the numbers suggest. In new construction, a pocket door costs modestly more than a barn door and is the better door for rooms that need privacy. In a finished house, a barn door costs far less to install and is the realistic choice for pantries, closets, laundries and openings where sealing does not matter. Spend the money on the pocket door when the room genuinely needs a latching door.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the wall beside the opening is empty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pocket doors need an empty cavity roughly a door width beside the opening, with no plumbing, wiring or ductwork. Check what runs through that wall before choosing a pocket door, ideally by looking from the basement, attic or an access panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the header in a load-bearing wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pocket door's rough opening is a little over twice the door width, and the header must span all of it. In a load-bearing wall that header must be sized for the load, which usually needs an engineer or building department sign-off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting a barn door where you need privacy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A barn door hangs in front of the opening with gaps on every side and takes only hook or surface latches. On a shared bathroom or a bedroom off a living area, a pocket door or hinged door is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using long screws on a pocket door wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall and anything hung on a pocket wall must use screws short enough not to reach the cavity, or they scratch the door and can damage the split studs. Avoid hanging heavy shelves or TVs over the pocket entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting barn door track into drywall alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A barn door and hardware can exceed a hundred pounds and the load moves. Anchors in drywall will fail. Fix the track into studs or onto a backer board fixed across several studs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a pocket door or barn door better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pocket door is the better door: it closes against a jamb, takes a privacy lock and leaves the wall clear. A barn door is the easier project: it fits on finished walls without demolition. Choose a pocket door in new construction or where privacy matters, and a barn door for retrofits on pantries, closets and openings where sealing does not matter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pocket doors more expensive than regular doors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. HomeAdvisor's 2026 guide puts pocket door installation at about $550 to $3,300 in new construction, against $150 to $200 for a standard door, and Fixr lists a traditional interior door at about $350 to $1,100 installed against $800 to $2,500 for a pocket door. Retrofits into existing walls cost considerably more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a pocket door cost to install in an existing wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                HomeAdvisor's 2026 guide puts retrofitting a pocket door at roughly $1,550 to $4,200, with a 36 inch door at $1,550 to $2,950, and lists opening up a wall at $1,200 to $2,500 by itself. The final price depends on whether the wall is load-bearing and what wiring or plumbing has to move.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a pocket door in any wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The wall needs an empty cavity about a door width beside the opening, with no plumbing, wiring or ducts, and room for a header across a rough opening a little over twice the door width. Load-bearing walls need a properly sized header. A standard frame kit is designed for a 2 by 4 stud wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pocket doors block sound better than barn doors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, generally. A pocket door sits inside the opening and closes against a jamb, while a barn door hangs in front with gaps on every side. A pocket door is still not a sealed door, and hollow-core slabs pass sound readily, so for a quiet office a solid-core door helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pocket door be locked?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Pocket door privacy locks are designed for sliding into a pocket and latch against the jamb, much like a bathroom lock on a hinged door. Barn doors can only take hooks and surface-mounted latches, which signal privacy rather than secure the door.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pocket doors hard to maintain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Day to day they need little attention, but the hardware is hidden. Many frame kits let you lift the door off its hangers after removing a strip of trim to adjust or replace them. A bent track or damaged split stud inside the wall is the costly failure, so buy a well-rated frame kit and avoid hanging heavy items on the pocket wall.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

